{'slug': 'kobe-8-protro-shanghai-fireworks-snkrs', 'title': 'Nike lists the Kobe 8 Protro Shanghai Fireworks on SNKRS at $200', 'category': 'Nike', 'date': '2026-11-11', 'publish': '2026-11-11T18:00:00+00:00', 'description': "Nike's SNKRS launch page for the Laser Orange and Safety Orange Kobe 8 Protro, SKU IO6259-800, shows a $200 price and a U.S. commerce start of 11 November 2026 at 10:23 a.m. ET.", 'image': '/assets/news/kobe-8-protro-shanghai-fireworks-snkrs.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Hands lacing a grey suede unbranded sneaker, no logos or readable text', 'body': '<p>Nike\'s <a href="https://www.nike.com/launch/t/kobe-8-protro-shanghai-fireworks-laser-orange-and-safety-orange">SNKRS launch page</a> for the Kobe 8 Protro "Shanghai Fireworks" is live, priced at $200 and identified as SKU IO6259-800. Product data on that page lists a U.S. commerce start of 11 November 2026 at 15:23 UTC, or 10:23 a.m. ET, with a current and full price of $200.</p><p>Nike says the colorway, in Laser Orange and Safety Orange, originally appeared in 2013 and is now returning as a Protro model. The official description says the upper has "vibrant artwork depicting the brilliance of a fireworks display." The Protro version, Nike says, uses a Nike React foam insert, a soft and pliable synthetic upper, and grippy traction for cuts and acceleration. It is described as low, sleek and light.</p><p>The listing reviewed on 11 November is a U.S. SNKRS product page for this SKU at $200. Availability on the app or in stores can change, and a launch-page listing is not a guarantee that a given size remains in stock. Details above are taken from Nike\'s own SNKRS page.</p>', 'sources': [('Kobe 8 Protro Shanghai Fireworks Laser Orange and Safety Orange (IO6259-800) Release Date', 'https://www.nike.com/launch/t/kobe-8-protro-shanghai-fireworks-laser-orange-and-safety-orange', 'Nike SNKRS', '2026-11-11')]},

{'slug': 'nike-sb-dunk-ducks-of-a-feather-release-dates', 'title': 'Nike SB Dunk Low “Ducks of a Feather” dates: The Zoo in November, Shout! in December', 'category': 'Release Dates', 'date': '2026-11-11', 'publish': '2026-11-11T14:45:00+00:00', 'description': "Sneaker News reported on 9 November that Division Street's Ducks of a Feather collection will release The Zoo at Flight Club and on the GOAT app in late November, with Shout! following in December.", 'image': '/assets/news/nike-sb-dunk-ducks-of-a-feather-release-dates.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Boutique wall display of colourful unbranded sneakers, no logos or text', 'body': '<p>Division Street\'s Ducks of a Feather project is returning with two Nike SB Dunk Low colourways, "Shout!" and "The Zoo," and <a href="https://sneakernews.com/2026/11/09/nike-sb-dunk-low-ducks-of-a-feather-shout-zoo/">Sneaker News</a> published the staggered release dates on 9 November.</p><p>The Zoo is scheduled to launch at Flight Club Tokyo on 20 November, at Flight Club stores in Los Angeles, Miami and New York on 21 November, and on the GOAT app on 26 November, Thanksgiving Day. Shout! follows at Flight Club Tokyo on 4 December, at Flight Club Los Angeles, Miami and New York on 5 December, and on the GOAT app on 10 December. Sneaker News also said both styles will be available through select regional skate shops. (A quoted calendar in the same article lists Tokyo again among the 5 December Flight Club stops; the prose dates above are the ones in the body of the report.)</p><p>Sneaker News said the collection connects University of Oregon traditions with sneaker and pop-culture references, in a collaboration with GOAT and Flight Club. Shout! is described as taking cues from the 1978 film National Lampoon\'s Animal House, which was shot in Eugene and on the Oregon campus, and from the "Shout" tradition at Autzen Stadium. The report says the colourway draws on the 2008 Nike SB Dunk Low "Newcastle," swapping that shoe\'s blue Swoosh for green, with "SHOUT!" on the heel and other film-related details on the tongue label and insoles.</p><p>The Zoo, the report said, refers to the "Autzen Zoo" nickname for the stadium\'s crowd. It describes croc-embossed materials, faux fur uppers and a snakeskin leather Swoosh, with a stadium sketch and "Once a Duck, Always a Duck" on the sockliner, and a tongue-label ticket graphic numbered 9231967, a reference to 23 September 1967, the day Autzen Stadium opened.</p><p>As with earlier Ducks of a Feather projects, Sneaker News said proceeds are intended to benefit participating University of Oregon student-athletes. The article did not list a retail price. Dates and shops can still change, so the Flight Club, GOAT and skate-shop listings closer to each launch remain the places to confirm how to buy a pair.</p>', 'sources': [('When To Buy The Nike SB Dunk Low “Ducks Of A Feather”', 'https://sneakernews.com/2026/11/09/nike-sb-dunk-low-ducks-of-a-feather-shout-zoo/', 'Sneaker News', '2026-11-09')]},
{'slug': 'vinicius-nike-mercurial-vapor-17-laser-orange', 'title': "Vinícius Júnior's Laser Orange Mercurial Vapor 17 is set for 12 November at $270", 'category': 'Release Dates', 'date': '2026-11-11', 'publish': '2026-11-11T14:40:00+00:00', 'description': 'House of Heat said Nike has unveiled a Laser Orange and Fuchsia Flash Mercurial Vapor 17 player edition inspired by Brazilian funk, releasing on 12 November for $270 in the Elite FG version.', 'image': '/assets/news/vinicius-nike-mercurial-vapor-17-laser-orange.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Unbranded running shoes on a stack of plain shoe boxes, no logos or text', 'body': '<p>Nike has unveiled another Vinícius Júnior Mercurial Vapor 17, in Laser Orange, with a 12 November release, <a href="https://houseofheat.co/nike/vini-jr-mercurial-vapor-17-laser-orange-iq8048-800">House of Heat reported</a>. The Elite FG version is listed at $270, to sell on Nike.com and at select retailers. The SKU is given as IQ8048-800, in Laser Orange and Fuchsia Flash.</p><p>House of Heat said the boots draw on the Brazilian funk music that raised the Real Madrid forward in Rio de Janeiro. The Atomknit upper is described as a gradient from Laser Orange into a deeper Fuchsia Flash around the lower heel, with dark, purplish-black Swoosh logos carrying graphics that nod to Brazil. The footballer\'s logo appears on the collar and insole. "Acredite," the Portuguese word for "Believe," appears on the insole, and the report said the Portuguese word and its English translation also appear on the outsole.</p><p>The launch is Thursday, 12 November. House of Heat presented official product images credited to Nike. A scheduled drop can still move, and stock is not guaranteed in every size or region, so Nike\'s own listing is the place to confirm price and availability on release morning.</p><p>This pair is a player-edition football boot, not a lifestyle sneaker, and the $270 figure is for the Elite FG version as stated in the House of Heat report. Other silos or junior sizes, if they exist, were not priced in that article.</p>', 'sources': [("Vini Jr.'s “Laser Orange” Nike Mercurial Vapor 17 Is Inspired by Brazilian Funk", 'https://houseofheat.co/nike/vini-jr-mercurial-vapor-17-laser-orange-iq8048-800', 'House of Heat', '2026-11-11')]},

{'slug': 'shipping-delays-mail-order-rule', 'title': 'Sneaker Shipping Delays: What the FTC Mail Order Rule Requires', 'category': 'Guides', 'date': '2026-11-11', 'publish': '2026-11-11T13:40:00+00:00', 'description': 'Understand shipping promises, delay notices, cancellation rights and refunds under the FTC Mail, Internet, or Telephone Order Merchandise Rule for online sneaker orders.', 'image': '/assets/news/shipping-delays-mail-order-rule.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Warehouse aisle with pallet racks of plain cardboard boxes', 'body': '<p>A delayed sneaker shipment is not automatically a violation of federal law. The key questions are what the seller promised about shipping, whether it had a reasonable basis for that promise, and what it did once it learned the order could not ship on time. The Federal Trade Commission’s Mail, Internet, or Telephone Order Merchandise Rule, codified at 16 CFR Part 435, addresses those questions for covered orders placed online, by mail, or by telephone. (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-435">ecfr.gov</a>)</p><h2>The shipping deadline is not always 30 days</h2><p>Under the FTC rule, a seller must have a reasonable basis to expect shipment within the time it clearly and conspicuously states. If it gives no shipment time, the usual default is 30 days after receiving a properly completed order. That is not a blanket promise that every online sneaker purchase must arrive within 30 days. The rule concerns <strong>shipment</strong>, not delivery at the customer\'s door. Shipment means placing merchandise physically in the carrier\'s possession.</p><p>Suppose a shoe listing says “ships within five business days.” The seller needs a reasonable basis for that five-day representation. It cannot simply rely on the general 30-day default instead. Conversely, if an eligible order contains no shipping representation, the default period generally applies. A special 50-day default can apply when a customer applies to the seller for credit to pay for the merchandise when ordering; that is not a universal extension for credit-card purchases.</p><p>A delivery estimate is also worth distinguishing from a shipping promise. Nike\'s shipping guidance says it displays estimated delivery dates at checkout and sends tracking information when an order ships. An estimated arrival date and the FTC\'s shipment obligation answer different questions. Save both the checkout estimate and the seller\'s specific shipping language.</p><h2>What the seller must do when it cannot ship</h2><p>If the merchant learns that it cannot meet the applicable shipping deadline, it must offer the buyer a choice: consent to a delay or cancel for a prompt refund. The notice must arrive within the original promised shipping period, or within the applicable default period if no time was specified. A seller may instead cancel and refund the order, notifying the buyer as required.</p><p>The first delay notice generally must give a revised shipping date or say that no definite date can be provided. It must explain the cancellation-and-refund option and provide a way to exercise it at the seller\'s expense. When the revised date is indefinite, the notice must explain the reason for the delay and the buyer\'s continuing right to cancel before shipment after consenting to that indefinite delay.</p><p>The FTC distinguishes shorter and longer first extensions. For a definite revised shipping date no more than 30 days beyond the original deadline, the seller may treat silence as consent only if the notice correctly explains that consequence and meets the rule\'s conditions. For a longer or indefinite extension, silence generally cannot simply be treated as an open-ended agreement: the rule has an automatic-cancellation framework unless shipment or express consent occurs within the specified period.</p><h2>What happens if the new date slips too</h2><p>A merchant that cannot meet its revised definite shipping date must seek consent to another delay before that date. The FTC\'s guidance makes an important distinction: silence cannot count as consent to a further extension in a renewed delay notice. If the required consent is not obtained and the goods have not shipped, the order must be canceled and a prompt refund provided under the rule.</p><p>Consider a hypothetical sneaker order with a promised October 10 shipment. Before October 10, the retailer provides a compliant notice moving shipment to October 25 and explains the cancellation option. If it later cannot ship by October 25, it cannot simply assume the buyer accepts a second extension. It must follow the renewed-notice rules. The example illustrates the process; it does not establish that any particular sneaker retailer missed its obligations.</p><p>The FTC defines prompt refunds in detail. Depending on the original payment method and the circumstances, the rule specifies the form and timing of a refund; for qualifying refunds it includes a seven-working-day standard. That does not mean every bank or card statement must display the credit instantly. Keep the cancellation confirmation and compare it with the seller\'s refund communication.</p><h2>How to document a delayed sneaker order</h2><p>Keep the product page or order confirmation showing the shipping promise, the checkout date, the payment record, every delay notice, and the carrier tracking history. Record the difference between an order that has been accepted, a shipping label that has been created, and a package actually handed to a carrier. The FTC\'s definition of shipment is about possession by the carrier, not merely generating a label.</p><p>When contacting customer support, ask for the promised ship-by date, the new expected shipping date, and the method for canceling and obtaining a refund. If the seller says the item is backordered without a firm new date, ask for the required delay options rather than assuming you must wait indefinitely.</p><p>This federal rule is not a general right to return shoes after delivery because the fit is wrong or because resale prices changed. Those questions depend on the applicable return policy and other law. Nor does it mean that every late delivery is a rule violation: the seller may have shipped on time even if a carrier delivered late. Focus first on the seller\'s shipping representation and the actual handoff to the carrier.</p><h2>When the rule applies</h2><p>The rule covers merchandise ordered online, by mail, or by telephone, regardless of the way it was advertised (16 CFR 435.1). It contains defined exclusions, so unusual transaction structures may need closer analysis, but ordinary prepaid online sneaker orders will generally be within its scope.</p><p>For shoppers, the practical sequence is straightforward: preserve the promised shipping date, check whether the merchandise actually shipped, read any delay notice for a revised date and cancellation option, and retain records of any refund request. The rule regulates how sellers handle shipping promises and delays; it does not guarantee that limited-release inventory will become available.</p>', 'sources': [('16 CFR Part 435 (eCFR)', 'https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-435', 'ecfr.gov', '2026-11-11'), ('16 CFR 435.2 (eCFR)', 'https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-435/section-435.2', 'ecfr.gov', '2026-11-11'), ('Nike shipping guidance', 'https://www.nike.com/help/a/shipping-delivery/order-tracking', 'nike.com', '2026-11-11')]},
{'slug': 'snkrs-vs-retailers', 'title': 'SNKRS vs Retail Partners: How to Choose Where to Enter a Sneaker Drop', 'category': 'Nike', 'date': '2026-11-11', 'publish': '2026-11-11T14:10:00+00:00', 'description': 'Compare Nike SNKRS launches with retail-partner drops, including entry methods, Exclusive Access, shipping, returns and the seller responsible for your order.', 'image': '/assets/news/snkrs-vs-retailers.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Boutique wall display of colorful unbranded sneakers', 'body': '<p>A sneaker can be associated with a Nike SNKRS launch while also appearing through other stores. That does not make every purchasing route interchangeable. The useful comparison is not just which page shows the shoe, but who is taking the order, what entry method is offered, and which seller\'s shipping and return rules will apply. (<a href="https://www.nike.com/launch/t/inside-snkrs-exclusive-access?sitesrc=snkrsIosShare">nike.com</a>)</p><h2>Start by identifying the actual seller</h2><p>SNKRS is Nike\'s launch experience. A retail partner is a separate merchant when it sells inventory through its own checkout or store. If a shoe appears on both, you should treat the two offers as separate purchasing opportunities, not assume one entry registers you for the other. Check the actual website or app, the merchant named at checkout, the product details, and the terms attached to that transaction.</p><p>The seller distinction matters after a successful purchase. Nike publishes its own return policy and shipping guidance for Nike orders. A purchase from another merchant is governed by that merchant\'s applicable policies, not automatically by Nike\'s direct-order rules. Even when the shoes are identical, your order confirmation and customer-service route may be different.</p><p>Build a simple comparison before release day: the seller, the launch or entry opening time, whether an entry or checkout is required, whether you can select a size, the displayed price, the delivery estimate, and the return terms. Do not treat a screenshot from a release calendar as a substitute for the merchant\'s own product page.</p><h2>SNKRS does not use just one access method</h2><p>Nike describes Exclusive Access on SNKRS as an invite-only launch. It says selection criteria can change and that users cannot guarantee access by trying to reverse-engineer the process. Nike also describes Exclusive Draw as an invite-only randomized selection launch in which invited users receive the same amount of time to enter and selection starts after the draw closes.</p><p>Those descriptions show why the phrase “SNKRS drop” is not enough to tell a shopper exactly how a purchase will work. An invitation, a timed entry, and a normal purchase flow are not the same experience. Read the instructions shown for the specific product and launch rather than assuming every release is a first-come checkout or every entry guarantees a purchase.</p><p>Nike advises users with Exclusive Access to keep SNKRS installed and push notifications enabled because invitations are time-limited. That is a useful precaution for Nike\'s own launch experience. It does not imply that another retailer\'s email, raffle, or in-store procedure will be synchronized with Nike\'s notifications.</p><h2>Compare opportunities without assuming inventory is shared</h2><p>Imagine a shoe advertised at a hypothetical $180 retail price on two sites. On one, Nike offers an invitation-only opportunity; on the other, a retailer announces its own entry period. Those are two distinct sets of instructions. The price may match while the selection process, fulfillment date, tax, shipping charge, and eligibility conditions differ. The $180 figure is an example, not a price claim about an actual release.</p><p>A retailer\'s listing is not proof that its available sizes, entry deadline, or stock quantity match Nike\'s. Likewise, an unsuccessful entry in one channel says nothing definite about your eligibility or chances in another. There is no sound basis to calculate a universal win rate across stores without release-specific entry and allocation data.</p><p>Rather than chasing a purported secret advantage, prioritize official information. Record the product name and identifying details from each listing. Check whether the launch is online, app-based, or in person. Read whether the merchant asks you to enter during a window or complete a purchase immediately. Follow the seller\'s posted rules about eligibility and duplicate entries.</p><h2>After a successful entry, look at the order status</h2><p>An invitation to enter is not the same thing as an order that has shipped. Nike\'s shipping guidance says it provides an estimated delivery date at checkout and sends a shipping confirmation email with tracking when the order ships. It also notes that orders can arrive in separate packages and through different carriers. Check your actual order status before interpreting a missing package as a canceled purchase.</p><p>For any retailer, preserve the receipt and confirmation number. Verify the charge against the merchant identified on the checkout page. If you have entered multiple opportunities, avoid assuming that one pending transaction is the result of a different entry. Keep the separate emails or app notifications organized by seller and product.</p><h2>Returns and cancellation are part of the comparison</h2><p>Nike\'s own return policy is the appropriate source for Nike direct purchases. A retail partner may set different eligibility requirements, time windows, or procedures. A shopper should check those terms before committing to a limited release, especially if the order is for a gift or the size is uncertain. Do not infer a retailer\'s policy from Nike\'s help center.</p><p>The comparison also includes the cost of a mistake. A shoe advertised at the same base price may produce different totals after shipping and tax. A policy that restricts returns on a particular product may make an otherwise attractive entry less useful. Read the terms for that exact purchase, including any launch-specific exceptions, rather than relying on general memories of past drops.</p><h2>A practical release-day decision</h2><p>If you have time to prepare for only one channel, choose the one whose eligibility rules, checkout method, and post-purchase policies you understand. If you use more than one legitimate channel, maintain a separate record of each entry and avoid breaching the merchant\'s rules. Treat an Exclusive Access notification as a time-sensitive opportunity, not a guarantee of a shoe.</p><p>The distinction is simple but consequential: Nike controls the SNKRS experience and its direct orders; an independent retail partner controls its own sales process. Comparing those processes carefully is more useful than assuming a product listing at multiple stores represents one common raffle.</p>', 'sources': [('Nike SNKRS Exclusive Access explanation', 'https://www.nike.com/launch/t/inside-snkrs-exclusive-access?sitesrc=snkrsIosShare', 'nike.com', '2026-11-11'), ('Nike return policy', 'https://www.nike.com/help/a/returns-policy', 'nike.com', '2026-11-11'), ('Nike shipping guidance', 'https://www.nike.com/help/a/shipping-delivery/order-tracking', 'nike.com', '2026-11-11')]},

{'slug': 'nike-skims-waffle-racer-november-10', 'title': "NikeSKIMS Waffle Racer drops today in three women's colorways at $130", 'category': 'Nike', 'date': '2026-11-10', 'publish': '2026-11-10T13:40:00+00:00', 'description': "Three women's NikeSKIMS Waffle Racer colorways are listed for 10 a.m. ET on Tuesday, November 10, at $130 each, with first come sales at one store.", 'image': '/assets/news/nike-skims-waffle-racer-november-10.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Flat lay of three pairs of pastel unbranded sneakers', 'body': '<p>Three women\'s colorways of the NikeSKIMS Waffle Racer are scheduled to release today, Tuesday, November 10, at 10 a.m. Eastern, according to the <a href="https://justdrops.com/this-week">Just Drops weekly calendar</a>. Each pair is listed at $130.</p><h2>The three colorways</h2><p>- Baroque Brown/Velvet Brown - Light Bone/College Grey - Thunder Blue/Blue Eclipse</p><p>All three share the same 10 a.m. Eastern slot, which is 9 a.m. Central, 8 a.m. Mountain and 7 a.m. Pacific.</p><h2>Where to look</h2><p>Just Drops lists first come, first served sales at one store for the Baroque Brown/Velvet Brown pair and has not published a store or app method for the other two colorways yet. That makes Nike\'s own channels and the NikeSKIMS collection pages the first places to check at drop time. The <a href="https://justdrops.com/today">today page</a> is updated through the morning, so look there for any late changes to the store list.</p><h2>How to approach a three colour drop</h2><p>With three versions landing at the same minute, picking one colorway in advance gives you the best chance of checking out before sizes sell through. These are women\'s sizes, so shoppers who usually buy men\'s sizes should convert first.</p><h2>Also out today</h2><p>The NikeSKIMS release shares the Tuesday slate with the Nike Air Max 95 Big Bubble "ACG" at $190 and the Nike Mind 002 Flyknit in Black/Anthracite at $145, both also at 10 a.m. Eastern on SNKRS. Wednesday brings the women\'s Air Jordan 11 "Magic Ball" and the <a href="/news/kobe-8-protro-shanghai-fireworks-snkrs/">Kobe 8 Protro "Shanghai Fireworks"</a>, which Nike later listed on SNKRS at $200.</p><h2>Before you pay</h2><p>Release calendars can change at short notice. Confirm the colorway name and price on the retailer\'s product page and buy only from Nike or a store you recognise.</p><p>RestockRally will update this page if the store list changes.</p>', 'sources': [('Sneaker Releases This Week: Nov 9 to Nov 15, 2026', 'https://justdrops.com/this-week', 'Just Drops', '2026-11-10'), ('Sneaker releases today', 'https://justdrops.com/today', 'Just Drops', '2026-11-10')]},

{'slug': 'jordan-son-of-mars-low-bred-preview', 'title': 'Jordan Son of Mars Low "Bred" preview: black and Fire Red, style JA4152-010', 'category': 'Jordan', 'date': '2026-11-09', 'publish': '2026-11-09T12:00:00+00:00', 'description': 'Sneaker News details an upcoming Jordan Son of Mars Low in a black-and-Fire Red Bred treatment tied to the Air Jordan 4 Bred cycle. Retail is listed at $165; no firm drop day yet.', 'image': '/assets/news/jordan-son-of-mars-low-bred-preview.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Nearly empty grocery endcap with a few plain silver cans left', 'body': '<p><a href="https://sneakernews.com/2026/11/08/jordan-son-of-mars-low-black-fire-red-cement-grey-ja4152-010/">Sneaker News</a> has detailed an upcoming Jordan Son of Mars Low in a black-and-Fire Red treatment associated with the Bred colour family. The outlet positions the shoe alongside the expected return of the Air Jordan 4 Bred, but it does not give a firm day for the Son of Mars Low release. This is a product preview, not a verified report of a November 9 restock or a completed sell-out.</p><h2>Construction and style code</h2><p>The report describes black nubuck and patent leather across the upper, with Fire Red accents on the midsole, inner tongue and eyestay. The Son of Mars Low is a hybrid that references elements of the Air Jordan 3, 4, 5 and 6. Sneaker News identifies the pair by style code <strong>JA4152-010</strong>, which is the detail to use when comparing retailer listings.</p><h2>Price and availability limits</h2><p>The outlet lists a <strong>$165</strong> retail price in men\'s sizing and says the pair is expected through Nike and selected retailers. Its buying panel gives 2026 as the release year rather than a specific date. No inventory quantity, launch time or confirmed retailer allocation is established. A promotional link or third-party widget is not a guarantee of stock, and published images do not prove the shoe has reached stores in a particular market.</p><h2>Why the distinction matters</h2><p>A coordinated colorway can draw attention around a more prominent retro release, but this source does not provide sales forecasts or evidence of unusual demand for the hybrid. The immediate development is that images, a style code and a reported price are available. A later retailer listing could settle the precise launch day and geographic availability. Until then, the right label is upcoming, not restocked.</p>', 'sources': [('The Jordan Son Of Mars Low Kickstarts The "Bred" Rollout', 'https://sneakernews.com/2026/11/08/jordan-son-of-mars-low-black-fire-red-cement-grey-ja4152-010/', 'Sneaker News', '2026-11-08')]},
{'slug': 'nike-air-force-1-mid-baroque-brown-preview', 'title': 'Nike Air Force 1 Mid "Baroque Brown" preview: IQ0189-200, $125', 'category': 'Nike', 'date': '2026-11-09', 'publish': '2026-11-09T12:30:00+00:00', 'description': 'Sneaker News publishes images of the Nike Air Force 1 Mid in Baroque Brown pebbled leather. Pairs are reportedly appearing overseas; a firm US launch day is not listed.', 'image': '/assets/news/nike-air-force-1-mid-baroque-brown-preview.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Open cardboard boxes on a conveyor with packing tape dispenser', 'body': '<p><a href="https://sneakernews.com/2026/11/08/nike-air-force-1-mid-baroque-brown-iq0189-200/">Sneaker News</a> has published images and details of the Nike Air Force 1 Mid in a Baroque Brown colorway, with pairs reportedly beginning to appear overseas. The outlet describes a Holiday 2026 release but does not provide a confirmed US launch date. Treat this as a product preview, not a claim that a nationwide restock is live.</p><h2>Design and identification</h2><p>The reported design uses a predominantly brown, pebbled full-grain leather upper with a silver lace dubrae as the main break from the tonal brown. Sneaker News identifies the style as <strong>IQ0189-200</strong>. The coverage places the colorway in the seasonal AF1 Mid lane that also includes finishes such as Flax. Those are design observations from the published product images, not evidence of demand or limited production.</p><h2>What the listing does and does not say</h2><p>Sneaker News displays a <strong>$125</strong> retail price and a 2026 release year in its buying information. The report says the shoe should reach Nike.com and other retailers, but it does not establish a specific US release day, inventory count or confirmed stock at every location. Reported overseas appearances also do not set the timing of domestic distribution.</p><h2>How to follow the release</h2><p>The clearest next confirmation is a live retailer product page showing the correct style code, sizes, price and shipping region. Until then, treat the date as pending. This colorway is a separate upcoming AF1 Mid story from this week\'s scheduled drops, and this report does not claim sell-outs or resale moves.</p>', 'sources': [('The Nike Air Force 1 Mid "Baroque Brown" Might Be The Ultimate Fall Shoe', 'https://sneakernews.com/2026/11/08/nike-air-force-1-mid-baroque-brown-iq0189-200/', 'Sneaker News', '2026-11-08')]},

{'slug': 'kith-new-balance-2011-monuments-release', 'title': 'Kith x New Balance 2011 "Monuments" collection releases November 9', 'category': 'New Balance', 'date': '2026-11-09', 'publish': '2026-11-09T13:00:00+00:00', 'description': "Ronnie Fieg's New York-inspired three-pair 2011 pack, in Park, Garden and Banner colorways, drops today at Kith and Kith.com. Garden is an in-store exclusive at New Balance at Kith West Hollywood.", 'image': '/assets/news/kith-new-balance-2011-monuments-release.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Illuminated glass display cases holding rows of sneakers in a dark boutique', 'body': '<p>Kith and New Balance are releasing a three-pair "Monuments" collection on the New Balance 2011 today, November 9, according to <a href="https://houseofheat.co/new-balance/kith-2011-monuments-collection">House of Heat</a>. The pack is inspired by New York City.</p><h2>Where to buy</h2><p>- <strong>Park and Banner:</strong> all Kith stores and Kith.com. - <strong>Garden:</strong> Kith.com, and in store only at New Balance at Kith West Hollywood.</p><p>House of Heat did not list retail prices, so check Kith\'s product pages for pricing and size availability.</p><h2>About the 2011</h2><p>Kith launched the New Balance 2011 earlier this year for its 15th anniversary. It is an original silhouette designed by Ronnie Fieg and New Balance. The multi-panel upper sits on ABZORB tooling taken from the New Balance 2002. The model\'s name refers to 2011, the year Kith was founded and its partnership with New Balance began.</p><h2>The three colorways</h2><p>- <strong>Park:</strong> builds on the palette of Fieg\'s 2014 New Balance 530. It is mostly maroon with Atomic Yellow and off-white accents, in pigskin suede and microperforated nubuck with quilting across the vamp and collar. - <strong>Garden:</strong> inspired by flower gardens. It pairs light grey with Amazon Green, lilac, Atomic Yellow and fuchsia, using microperforated pigskin suede, solid suede overlays and leather inserts. - <strong>Banner:</strong> a Princess Blue pair with silver and orange accents. It has a mesh vamp and pigskin suede overlays.</p><h2>Details</h2><p>All three pairs have asymmetrical Kith and New Balance heel branding. They come in custom packaging illustrated with a map of New York City and its monuments, with matching artwork on the sockliners and two extra sets of laces.</p><p>Fieg has also teased a fourth colorway, which House of Heat expects to stay a friends-and-family exclusive.</p>', 'sources': [('Kith and New Balance Celebrate NYC With the 2011 "Monuments" Collection', 'https://houseofheat.co/new-balance/kith-2011-monuments-collection', 'House of Heat', '2026-11-06')]},

{'slug': 'nigo-j-hope-nike-air-force-1-burnt-sunrise', 'title': "Sole Links: NIGO x J-Hope Air Force 1 '01 down to a few sizes at End", 'category': 'Restocks', 'date': '2026-11-08', 'publish': '2026-11-08T13:30:00+00:00', 'description': "Sole Links flagged on 8 November 2026 that the NIGO x J-Hope x Nike Air Force 1 '01 in Burnt Sunrise (style code IO8766-800) was down to a few sizes at End.", 'image': '/assets/news/nigo-j-hope-nike-air-force-1-burnt-sunrise.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Unbranded white leather low-top sneakers on a concrete plinth', 'body': '<p>The NIGO x J-Hope x Nike Air Force 1 \'01 was down to a few sizes at End on Sunday morning, according to Sole Links.</p><p>The <a href="https://x.com/SOLELINKS/status/2119405574282785014">8 November 2026 post</a>, labelled as an ad, read: "Few sizes via End. NIGO x J-HOPE x Nike Air Force 1 \'01", with a link to the retailer\'s product page.</p><h2>What the shoe is</h2><p><a href="https://www.endclothing.com/en-us/products/nike-x-nigo-x-j-hope-air-force-1-01-sneaker-io8766-800">End\'s product page</a> lists the pair as the Nike x Nigo x J-Hope Air Force 1 \'01 in Burnt Sunrise, style code IO8766-800. The retailer describes Burnt Sunrise leather with metallic gold overlays and squirrel graphics on the heel and sockliner, a nod to the BTS member\'s "hobicore" style, and says the design looks back to early-1990s Tokyo.</p><h2>How Restock Rally labels this</h2><p>Sole Links posts are affiliate-style availability alerts, not Nike or End inventory statements. "Few sizes" means the account saw limited stock when it posted; sizes can sell through within minutes. Check End\'s page yourself for current sizes and price before you decide, and be wary of resale listings well above retail.</p><p>Primary chatter: Sole Links\' <a href="https://x.com/SOLELINKS/status/2119405574282785014">few-sizes alert</a>. Retail listing: <a href="https://www.endclothing.com/en-us/products/nike-x-nigo-x-j-hope-air-force-1-01-sneaker-io8766-800">End</a>.</p>', 'sources': [("SOLELINKS on X: few sizes via End, NIGO x J-HOPE x Nike Air Force 1 '01", 'https://x.com/SOLELINKS/status/2119405574282785014', 'SOLELINKS on X', '2026-11-08'), ("Nike x Nigo x J-Hope Air Force 1 '01 Sneaker Burnt Sunrise", 'https://www.endclothing.com/en-us/products/nike-x-nigo-x-j-hope-air-force-1-01-sneaker-io8766-800', 'END. Clothing', '2026-11-08')]},
{'slug': 'air-max-90-triple-black-below-retail-november-13', 'title': 'Air Max 90 Triple Black already listed below $165 retail ahead of November 13 release', 'category': 'Nike', 'date': '2026-11-08', 'publish': '2026-11-08T12:20:00+00:00', 'description': 'Resale tracker claqa found the Nike Air Max 90 Triple Black listed under its $165 retail in every size from 8 to 12 before its 13 November release, from $131.', 'image': '/assets/news/air-max-90-triple-black-below-retail-november-13.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Unbranded running shoes on a stack of plain shoe boxes', 'body': '<p>The Nike Air Max 90 "Triple Black" is already listed for less than its $165 retail price ahead of its Friday, 13 November release, according to <a href="https://claqa.com/releases/1183/sneaker-releases-this-week-november-9-2026">claqa\'s 7 November price check</a>. The resale tracker found listings under retail in every US size from 8 to 12, starting at $131.</p><h2>Falling before launch</h2><p>A week earlier the cheapest listing was $161, claqa said, so the lowest asking price has dropped about 19% in seven days. Its figures are the lowest listing for a new pair with the original box, by US size, and pre-release prices can move quickly.</p><h2>A busy Friday</h2><p>The Triple Black is one of two Air Max 90s on claqa\'s 13 November calendar, alongside the "Medium Olive Sequoia" at $170, which had no listings yet. The same day also brings the Air Diamond Turf "Patent Black" ($170), the New Balance Abzorb 1890 "Altitude Blue Black" ($180), the Nike KD 19 "Aunt Pearl" ($170) and the Air Zoom G.T. Cut "Black Volt" ($190), among others.</p><h2>What it means for buyers</h2><p>claqa\'s editor wrote that anyone who wants the Triple Black in sizes 8 to 12 could buy now, since it is already under retail in all of them. Asking prices are not completed sales, and fees, shipping and tax can close the gap with a retailer checkout. Check current listings and the retail release page before you buy.</p>', 'sources': [('Sneaker releases Nov 9 to Nov 15: a packed Friday, and two pairs already under retail', 'https://claqa.com/releases/1183/sneaker-releases-this-week-november-9-2026', 'claqa', '2026-11-07')]},
{'slug': 'new-balance-abzorb-1890-altitude-blue-black-november-13', 'title': 'New Balance Abzorb 1890 Altitude Blue Black listed at $392 before November 13 release', 'category': 'New Balance', 'date': '2026-11-08', 'publish': '2026-11-08T12:00:00+00:00', 'description': 'Resale tracker claqa found the New Balance Abzorb 1890 Altitude Blue Black listed at $392 in every size, $212 over its $180 retail, ahead of its 13 November release.', 'image': '/assets/news/new-balance-abzorb-1890-altitude-blue-black-november-13.jpg', 'image_alt': 'Side profile of a grey mesh running shoe with an orange midsole', 'body': '<p>The New Balance Abzorb 1890 "Altitude Blue Black" is listed at $392 in every size ahead of its Friday, 13 November release, according to <a href="https://claqa.com/releases/1183/sneaker-releases-this-week-november-9-2026">claqa\'s 7 November price check</a>. With a $180 retail price, that is $212 over retail.</p><h2>An early premium on a busy day</h2><p>The Abzorb is one of seven releases claqa lists for 13 November, a day that also includes two Air Max 90s, the Nike KD 19 "Aunt Pearl" and the Air Zoom G.T. Cut "Black Volt". It contrasts with the Air Max 90 "Triple Black" on the same calendar, which is already listed under retail.</p><p>claqa\'s prices are the lowest listing for a new pair with the original box, by US size. A flat $392 across every size suggests a thin pre-release market rather than settled demand.</p><h2>A snapshot, not a sale</h2><p>Asking prices are not completed sales, and pre-release listings can change sharply once pairs ship. The release date and $180 retail come from claqa\'s calendar; check New Balance and authorised retailers for regional timing and final pricing, and factor in platform fees, shipping and authentication before paying a premium.</p>', 'sources': [('Sneaker releases Nov 9 to Nov 15: a packed Friday, and two pairs already under retail', 'https://claqa.com/releases/1183/sneaker-releases-this-week-november-9-2026', 'claqa', '2026-11-07')]},

